<tr><td style="padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">“What we are hearing from clients is not fear, it is fatigue,” said one rate strategist at a large dealer bank, speaking on condition of anonymity because he is not authorized to talk to press. “Everyone wants the all-clear signal, and nobody wants to be the one who says it too early.”</p></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">Equity breadth, meanwhile, was ugly. Roughly a third of constituents closed lower even as the headline index rose, which is what a narrowing market looks like before it stops being a narrowing market.</p></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">For the retail investor, none of this is actionable on its own. It is, however, a reminder that a portfolio built for a world of falling rates is a portfolio with one assumption holding it up, and assumptions are cheaper to test than to repair.</p></td></tr><tr><td class="footer" style="padding:24px;font-size:12px;color:#9aa;line-height:18px;">
